the Food and Drug Administration is  ordering a Las Vegas company to recall  all of its dietary supplements  containing the ingredient kratom it is  linked to a nationwide salmonella  outbreak the FDA says this is its first  mandatory recall for a tainted food  product the agency says 26 items with  kratom from four companies including the  one from Las Vegas tested positive for  salmonella  there are nearly 90 reported infections  in 35 states and a war nurse here with  the concerns over kratom and a good  morning good morning well some consumers  argue that kratom can be an alternative  pain medicine or a treatment for opioid  withdrawal but the FDA says kratom has  not been proven safe and effective and  the salmonella outbreak is adding to  existing concerns over loosely regulated  dietary supplements when I first started  reading about it it sounded like snake  oil to me Andrew Turner is a military  veteran who turned to kratom three years  ago to help with pain from service  injuries and PTSD pharmaceutical  solutions were not working in my  situation and I was steadily  deteriorating Turner uses kratom powder  to make tea he buys it online from a  company he says follows the strictest  manufacturing and testing standards and  shares those test results with customers  since early March three companies that  sell kratom products have issued  voluntary recalls that the FDA's request  due to salmonella contamination but on  Tuesday the agency issued its first  mandatory recall order for all of  triangle Pharma naturals LLC's food  products containing kratom FDA  Commissioner Scott Gottlieb said in a  statement this action is based on the  imminent health risk posed by the  contamination of this product with  Salmonella and the refusal of this  company to voluntarily act we commend  the FDA for taking swift action to  protect the public Chuck Bell is the  program's director for consumers Union  the policy division of Consumer Reports  he says dietary supplements like kratom  run the risk of containing contaminated  ingredients the issue is that they're  not subject to pre market safety testing  so unlike prescription drugs they've not  been shown to be either safe or  effective but Turner says he were  tougher regulations on kratom may cut  off access people should be able to make  a decision for themselves and weigh the  risks versus the benefits look at the  research that's available out there the  FDA is advising consumers to avoid  kratom and kratom containing products  the agency says it will continue working  with the Centers for Disease Control to  investigate the ongoing outbreak  triangle Pharma Naturals did not respond  to our request for comments we were  unable to reach them but there's a lot  of kratom fans out there too who swear  by it they don't want it off the market  interesting that the FDA's first  mandatory recalls yeah it is thank you  very much Anna
 

